# Untitled

New Nanophotonics Approaches for Enhancing the Efficiency and Stability of Perovskite Solar Cells
Over the past decade, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has experienced a remarkable ascent, soaring from \(3.8\%\) in 2009 to a remarkable record of \(26.1\%\) in 2023. Many recent approaches for improving PSC performance employ nanophotonic technologies, from light harvesting and thermal management to the manipulation of charge carrier dynamics. Plasmonic nanoparticles and arrayed dielectric nanostructures have been applied to tailor the light absorption, scattering, and conversion, as well as the heat dissipation within PSCs to improve their PCE and operational stability. In this review, it is begin with a concise introduction to define the realm of nanophotonics by focusing on the nanoscale interactions between light and surface plasmons or dielectric photonic structures. Prevailing strategies that utilize resonance-enhanced light–matter interactions for boosting the PCE and stability of PSCs from light trapping, carrier transportation, and thermal management perspectives are then elaborated, and the resultant practical applications, such as semitransparent photovoltaics, colored PSCs, and smart perovskite windows are discussed. Finally, the state-of-the-art nanophotonic paradigms in PSCs are reviewed, and the benefits of these approaches in improving the aesthetic effects and energy-saving character of PSC-integrated buildings are highlighted.
## 1. Introduction
Global challenges such as energy crisis,[1,2] global warming,[3–5] and extreme weather[6–9] have forced the human beings to look into clean and sustainable alternative energy solutions.[10–15] The development of renewable energy can effectively alleviate these challenges.[12,16–20] However, the utilizations of renewable and clean energies such as wind power, hydroelectric power and nuclear power are either highly dependent on geographic conditions or have huge potential risks.[21–24] Fortunately, the effective utilization of solar energy through photovoltaics (PV) technologies could overcome all the abovementioned limitations, because solar cells (SCs) can be mounted at any locations with solar irradiation to directly convert light into electricity, without too much geographical restrictions or potential risks in nuclear radiation.[25–27] Motivated by such a distinct advantage, crystalline silicon (Si) SCs, pioneered since the mid-20th century,[28] have demonstrated commendable efficiency
over \(26\%\) under one-sun illumination,[29,30] yet their production is hamstrung by the exorbitant cost associated with the rigorous purification, high-temperature processing, and harsh manufacturing conditions of crystalline Si. The high cost of Si production and the resultant carbon footprint restrain the sustainable production and more scalable applications of Si SCs.[31,32]
Since the year of 2009, Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted a great deal of attention from both the scientific and technological community due to their ever-growing PCE, low-cost raw materials, thin-film structures and facile fabrication processes.[20,33–43] The PCE of single-junction PSCs has grown from the initial \(3.8\%\) in the year of 2009 to the latest more than \(26\%\), approaching the theoretical limit efficiency of single-junction PSCs,[35,43,44] and rivaling other existed PV semiconductors, such as multi(mono)-crystalline Si,[29,30] dye-sensitized,[45,46] gallium arsenide (GaAs),[47,48] copper indium gallium selenide (CIGS),[49,50] as well as quantum dot (QD) SCs.[51,52] However, PSCs still suffer from questionable stability under long-term operation condition,[34,36,41,53,54] rendering PSC commercialization. This poses a major challenge for PSCs to compete with the existing inorganic PV technologies.[34,53] The major factors that affect the long-term durability during PSC operation include light, moisture and heat. For example, ultraviolet (UV) light
will cause desorption of surface-adsorbed oxygen in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) charge-transporting layer in PSCs, creating electronic trap sites and hence lowering the efficiency of PSCs.[55] Moisture that triggers chemical decomposition of perovskites has been always considered as a main degradation pathway of PSCs when exposed in the ambient conditions without encapsulation.[54,56] Continuous irradiation-induced heating \((>\!60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) that causes the phase separation,[57,58] ion migration,[59] halide segregation,[60–63] strain accumulation,[64–66] etc.,[53,67] represents the most challenging issue, which inevitably hinders the commercialization of PSCs.
Nanophotonics strategy has been emerging as an advanced technology to manipulate photon behavior and realize efficient light management for high-efficiency and stable perovskite optoelectronics.[68–74] Compared to nanophotonics methods, the commonly used strategies for enhancing the efficiency and stability of PSCs mainly focus on: 1) the introduction of additives for optimizing perovskite crystalline to reduce the intrinsic trap density, so that the electrons and holes can be effectively generated upon photo-excitation, as well as stabilizing perovskite structure for enhancing the long-term stability;[75–79] 2) passivation strategies, including surface, interface, and bulk passivation channels, can diminish the influence of the defects, and boost ambient tolerance (such as light, oxygen, and moisture) attributing to the strong chemical bonds, suppression of nonradiative recombination, strain relaxation, or smooth carrier transportation;[36,41,80–83] 3) the optimization of electron-transporting layer (ETL) and hole-transporting layer (HTL) can facilitate electron and hole extraction, transportation and collection processes, thus improving the PCE of PSCs.[84–86] 4) the regulation of interfacial energy level alignment between different functional layers in PSCs for promoting the electrons and holes to be smoothly transported toward the anode and cathode of the SCs, respectively, and therefore maximizing the open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{OC}})\) and \(\mathrm{PCE}\);[87–92] These methods have made respectable achievements, yet a summary of new nanophotonics approaches for boosting the efficiency and stability of PSCs from fundamental nanophotonic principle to comprehensive photovoltaics gains in light trapping, carrier transportation, and thermal management perspectives is rarely reported.
In this article, we concentrate on reviewing the light–matter interactions of plasmonic and dielectric nanostructures from the perspective of resonance-enhanced light absorption, scattering and conversion, and summarize the latest nanophotonics approaches for improving the performance and stability of PSCs, as well as putting forward the challenges and opportunities when applied them in PSCs, as illustrated in Figure 1. In the beginning, we introduce the scope and concept of nanophotonics, the generation and relaxation pathways of plasmons, and light management enabled by the periodic dielectric photonic structures. Especially, we highlight the mechanisms of the latter two sections on increasing the efficiency and stability of PSCs. Subsequently, we systematically summarize the latest nanophotonics methods to improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs. Next, we also provide a summary of the opportunities and challenges of nanophotonic methods on improving the efficiency and stability of PSCs, and propose potential solutions for the challenges. Finally, we present an outlook by combining the existing methods to maximize the efficiency and stability of PSCs. For example, integrating electrochromic window with semi-transparent PSCs together
is a promising technology for a new generation of self-powered smart energy-saving windows; developing both high transmission (below the cutoff wavelengths of the PSCs, or even the whole solar spectrum) and high emission (in atmospheric window, 8– \(13\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) radiative cooling materials on the PSCs ensures that they can be kept cool during practical operation to extend the lifetime of PSCs.
## 2. Concepts and Principles Of Nanophotonics
## 2.1. The Concept and Scope of Nanophotonics
Nanophotonics is a subject focusing on investigating the behavior of light and light–matter interaction at the nanometer scale.[95–97] It generally studies the metallic nanostructures, especially for the noble metal nanostructures, which can generate, propagate and relax plasmons under the illumination of light.[98,99] By carefully manipulating the size, shape, composition, and dielectric environment of the nanostructures, the desirable optical properties,[100–106] such as scattering enhancement, photoluminescence (PL), second harmonic generation (SHG), and two-phonon photoluminescence (TPL), can be precisely tailored to improve the efficiency of light management. In addition, periodic dielectric photonic nanostructures can also be properly applied for efficient light management and light-to-heat management.
## 2.2. Light-Plasmonic Nanostructure Interaction
## 2.2.1. Excitation and Generation of Plasmons
When the frequency of the incident excitation wavelength is equal to the oscillation frequency of the free electron within the metal nanostructures, the strongly coherent resonance between the photons and the electrons will induce the collective electron oscillations, and therefore produce plasmons.[107] In general, surface plasmons can be excited in a broad range of plasmonic metallic nanostructures with specific resonance wavelengths, depending on their sizes, shapes, compositions and dielectric surroundings.[100–102] Figure 2a–c shows the three typical types of light–metallic nanostructure interactions that generate surface plasmons, including light–metal NP monomer, light–metal NP dimer and light–metal grating electrode excitations. As the normal incident light illuminates on a metal NP monomer, the light–metal NP monomer interaction (Figure 2a) can induce localized surface plasmon (LSP) at the both ends of nanosphere. When another metal NP monomer is introduced into this system and they form a metal NP dimer (Figure 2b), the dimer will not only produce LSP at its both ends, but also will generate hybrid LSP between the two monomers. In addition, metal electrode grating with the periodic nanostructure (Figure 2c), usually applied as anode or cathode of PSCs,[108,109] can also excite LSPs at the metal surface and propagating surface plasmon polaritons (SPPs) along the propagating direction.[110–112]
## 2.2.2. Decay Mechanisms of Plasmons
The photon energy is absorbed by the plasmonic nanostructures and arises plasmon resonances, which then decay mainly
by emitting a phonon in a radiative manner (Figure 2d) or generating hot electron-hole pairs in a nonradiative manner (Figure 2e–g).[93,98,113] The decisive factor for a radiative or nonradiative way depends on the geometry size of the plasmonic NP. The large size of the plasmonic NPs (e.g., \(>50\,\mathrm{nm}\) for Ag NP) tends to exhibit a radiative behavior in 10 fs, which has attracted lots of attention in surface enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS),[114,115] scanning near-field optical microscope (SNOM),[116,117] florescence imaging,[118–120] and quantum emitters.[121] While the small size of the plasmonic NPs often shows a nonradiative one, which leads to a broad range of applications as well, such as interfacial solar desalination,[122–124] ultrafast photodetector,[125] pyrocatalytic hydrogen generation,[126] improving performance of PSCs,[127–129] and solar-thermoelectric conversion.[18] During the nonradiative relaxation,[93,98,99,113] a plasmon quantum is converted into a pair of hot electron and hot hole via Landau damping in the time scale of 1–100 fs (Figure 2e). A possible distribution of hot carrier population is presented in the bottom of Figure 2e.[98] Then the excited-state hot electrons are transferred their energy to the low-energy electrons to redistribute their energy (in the bottom of Figure 2f) by an electron–electron scattering process within a time frame of 100 fs-1 ps. Subsequently, the residual hot carriers continue to shift energy to the plasmonic metal NP through an electron–phonon scattering process within 1 ps to 100 ps (local heating). At last, the plasmonic NP keeps the thermal equilibrium with the surrounding by a phonon–phonon interaction process (Figure 2g). This process can take 100 ps to 10 ps to reach the equilibrium depending on the nature of the plasmonic NP. It is particularly noted that the radiative process and nonradiative process can happen in the light-induced plasmonic nanostructures at the same time.[93] Hence, tailoring the relaxation properties to the desired applications is of great significance.
