## 2.2.3. Plasmon-Induced Radiative Decay
As discussed in the above, the excited-state plasmon can release a photon by a radiative relaxation process. The released photon energy is either linear or nonlinear with respect to the excitation
photon energy. For example, one incoming photon interacts with the plasmonic NP, and then the NP emits one photon with the same or lower energy by an elastic or inelastic process (in the left of Figure 3a). This is a linear optics behavior, which includes scattering, fluorescence and PL phenomenon. While two or more incident photons are absorbed by the plasmonic NP and only one photon with larger energy or two phonons with different energies is released in the afterward (in the right of Figure 3a).[130] It is a nonlinear process that is mainly applied for SHG and TPL spectroscopy.[131]
Both the linear process and nonlinear process associated with the radiative decay of surface plasmons can exhibit many interesting optical phenomenon, enabling a wide range of device applications in the emerging nanophotonics field.[95,136–138] Regarding the linear process, Lei et al. studied the plasmonic gap modes in a particle-on-film system (Figure 3b, top) and a particle-embedded-into-film system (Figure 3b, bottom) by the dark-field scattering spectroscopy.[132] Figure 3c,d shows the measured and simulated scattering spectra of the particle-on-film system with a nanometer-scale particle-film distance, showing that the scattering intensity is highly excitation-polarization-dependent and the maximum scattering intensity can be achieved under near-normal incident polarization (i.e., the \(90^{\circ}\) case). The dark-field optical microscopy (OM) images between Figure 3c,d are also consistent with the above spectroscopic results. Figure 3e,f present similar excitation-polarization-dependent results for the particle-embedded-into-film system, revealing that the long-wavelength scattering peak is related with a vertically aligned dipolar mode that exhibits distinctively different dipole orientation and damping rate from the short-wavelength peak. The plasmonic gap mode observed in the particle-on-film system (Figure 3g) can also be used to enhance the PL of metal Kik et al. reported that a more remarkable near-field enhancement effect (Figure 3h) can be observed near the resonance wavelength (652 nm) of the system than that far away from the resonance wavelength by simulation.[133] When 532 nm and 633 nm laser were used to excite the plasmonic system, the obvious PL emissions were observed in the both experiments (Figure 3i), and the latter exhibited higher PL intensity than the former, which were also proved by the simulated PL spectra (Figure 3j). The gap mode in particle-on-film configuration can not only be applied in the linear optics, but also can be utilized to induce SHG in nonlinear optics. One of a pioneered applications of light-induced symmetry breaking for enhancing SHG by the gap mode in particle-on-film configuration was reported by Li and colleagues,[134] where Au NP coated with 1 nm CTAB (cetyltrimethylammonium bromide) dielectric layer was placed on the Au film (Figure 3k) and light was used to excite SHG by the configuration called a virtual
