nanoparticle dimer (VND). The excitation mechanism was explained in Figure 3l, in which double photons with the equal frequency interact with the configuration and produce one photon with the double frequency of initial photon. The single VNDs exhibit a distinct SH emission peak, which is accompanied by a broad TPL peak of comparable intensity (Figure \(3\mathrm{m}\) ). When the incident wavelength is matched with plasmon resonance wavelength, the proposed configuration shows a much higher \((\approx\!13.5\) -fold) SH intensity signal than a real nanoparticle dimer (RND) with weak asymmetry (Figure 3n). This is because lightinduced symmetric breaking within the gap mode promotes the SHG and SH signal is further enlarged by the directional coupling of the nanocavity, which offers new possibilities for the nonlinear nanophotonics device such as ultrafast on-chip optical switches.[139]
In addition to the plasmonic gap modes in particle-on-film systems, other plasmonic metal nanostructures are able to exhibit various linear and nonlinear optical behaviors.[98,99,130,134,140–142] For example, aluminum (Al) nanosheets fabricated in the presence of oxygen by Luo and his co-authors showed adjustable scattering property and excellent TPL phenomenon.[135] Figure 3o illustrated the experimental configuration of TPL measurements, where laser went through a quartz cuvette containing the different thicknesses of Al nanosheets and high-energy photons compared to the laser energy are detected. The corolla-like structures presented in Figure 3p was the Al nanosheets. The scattering property of Al nanosheets was regulated by the thickness of nanosheet. As shown in Figure 3q, the localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) peaks shifted from 810 to \(632\,\mathrm{nm}\) as the thickness of Al nanosheets increased from 2 to \(10\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which was in line with the simulation results. Afterward, the authors applied \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\) fs-laser to excite the Al nanosheets, the Al nanosheet with a thickness of \(2~\mathrm{nm}\) exhibited the strongest TPL intensity among the other samples, demonstrating that Al nanosheets can simultaneously behave superior linear and nonlinear properties, as well as can enable a wide range of applications.[18,143–145]
## 2.2.4. Plasmon-Induced Nonradiative Decay
As for plasmon-excited nonradiative relaxation process, it is a much more complicated course than the radiative one. To the best of our knowledge, the whole nonradiative relaxation process includes at least three stages, i.e., hot-carrier generation, hot-carrier relaxation and local heating. Since the whole process only takes place within 10 ns,[98] it requires very advanced tools for characterizations. Thanks to the fast development of ultrafast optics, fs \((10^{-15}\) s) laser and even atosecond \((10^{-18}\) s) have already been applied to detect the nonradiative decay process, allowing the research community to gain deeper understanding into these complex processes.[98,150–155]
Plasmon-Induced Hot Carrier Transfer: It is widely believed that the production and relaxation of hot-carrier can be tailored to specific properties to achieve desirable destinations (Figure 4a), such as filling trap states, neutralizing charged defects, promoting carrier transport in PSCs, as well as enhancing electron-hole separation in the catalysis field.
Normally, there are numerous interfacial trap states in the complicated multilayer structure of PSCs, which will serve as the main electron-hole recombination center and thus undermine the PCE of PSCs. Therefore, eliminating these trap states to improve the PCE is highly desirable. For example, mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) has been always been applied in PSCs to improve the conductivity of ETL; however, the low crystallinity of pristine \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) caused a lot of trap states and therefore inhibited the charge-transporting properties.[156] A sandwiched \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) (Figure 4b,d, denoted as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -Au in the initial literature) architecture proposed by Sun et al. was applied as electron conductor to enhance the carrier transport efficiency.[146] The plasmon-excited hot electrons, which pass through the \(\mathrm{Au-TiO_{x}}\) Schottky barrier and inject into the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) , not only directly filled the trap states in \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) , but also increased its conductivity and surface potential simultaneously, resulting in an obvious improvement of PCE (Figure 4d) and enlarged external quantum efficiency (EQE, Figure 4e). In addition, Yao et al.[128] introduced a kind of poly(3-aminothiophenol)- coated Au \((\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{PAT})\) NPs into the grain boundary of perovskite layer (Figure 4f) to investigate the influence of plasmon resonance on the transportation dynamics of the carriers trapped near the grain boundary. The cell structure is presented in Figure \(4\mathrm{g}\) . The authors measured the PCE of control PSC (Figure 4h, the control one) and PSC with \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{PAT}\) incorporation (Figure 4h, the target one), and the results showed the latter one exhibited a higher PCE of \(20.2\%\) than that of the former one of \(18.5\%\) . In addition, the durability of PSC with \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{PAT}\) incorporation was also remarkably improved (Figure 4i). In the initial paper, transient absorption (TA) spectra and density functional theory (DFT) investigations indicated that plasmon-induced hot-carrier transfer from plasmonic Au NPs to perovskite layer repaired the trap states at the grain boundary of perovskite, which ultimately improve the PCE of PSC with \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{PAT}\) up to \(22\%\) . Furthermore, poor carrier-transporting properties will also undermine the performance of PSCs, which also serves as one of most serious issues in PSCs. Cui et al.[147] also used a sandwiched \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -Au\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) as the ETL (Figure 4j,k), and plasmon-induced hot electrons transfer from Au NP to \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) largely promoted the local electron mobility in \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , causing significant increase in charge transport efficiency. As a result, the performance of PSCs with Au NPs was obviously improved (Figure 4l), and the champion PSC device remarkably increased the efficiency up to \(19.42\%\) (Figure \(4\mathrm{m}\) ). To go one step further, Ren and his colleagues incorporated spheroidal metal NPs in the perovskite active layer (Figure 4n) to alleviate the ohmic loss and temperature rise by
reasonable optical-electrical-thermal optimization.[148] The simulated results show that the incorporation of plasmonic NPs with optimal geometry in PSC devices can obviously increase the absorption by \(17\%\) compared with the device without metal NPs (Figure 4o). In addition, the optimal PSC not only exhibits low local temperature, but also shows improved carrier transportation dynamics. As a result, the PSC performance improves with increment in the \(J_{S C}\) by \(23\%\) and the PCE by \(38\%\).
Besides in PSC field, plasmon-induced hot carrier process has many other significant applications,[93,157–162] such as photocatalytic degradation for environmental remediation,[93,144,163–167] A typical plasmonic nanostructure \(\mathrm{Au-TiO}_{2}\) dumbbell (Figure 4p) was fabricated by Lei's group.[149] Under the visible light illumination, excited-state hot electrons generated from Au d-band were energy sufficient to migrate to the conduction band (CB) of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) through \(\mathrm{Au-TiO}_{2}\) Schottky junction, triggering the photochemical reaction. The steady-state photocurrent experiments indicated that \(\mathrm{Au/TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}\) with nanocavity structure showed more apparent photocurrent response at different wavelengths (800 and \(560\ \mathrm{nm}\)) than \(\mathrm{Au/TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}\) without nanocavity structure (Figure 4q), verifying that the Au film-mediated near field enhancement accelerate the electron–hole separation and transportation in the \(\mathrm{Au-TiO}_{2}\) dumbbell, and hence can be applied for enhancing kinetics of photocatalysis reaction.
Plasmon-Induced Local Heating: In addition, local heating (Figure 5a) caused by the plasmonic nanostructures can convert the absorbed light energy into heat via a phonon–phonon scattering path. It gradually attracts more and more attention in different research fields,[107,162,168–170] such as strain regulation,[129,171,172] reducing exciton binding energy,[173] improving the mobility of charge carriers,[174] promoting pyrocalytic hydrogen generation,[126] solar desalination,[122,175] etc.
The residual strain produced in the growth and operation stages of perovskite films is a common issue that will no doubt decrease the stability and efficiency of PSCs. In order to address this issue, Liu et al.[176] incorporated \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene between \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL and perovskite layer (Figure 5b) to regulate the tensile strain of PSCs and inhibit the Auger recombination. Figure 5c shows a significantly enhanced PCE after the \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene is introduced into the SC. According to the X-ray diffraction (XRD) results provided by the authors, the incorporation of \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene can greatly suppress the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) segregation under the continuous illumination. The control group without plasmonic NPs shows obviously perovskite degradation caused by the accumulation of compression strain. Therefore, the perovskite film with \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene (Figure 5d) exhibits more stable absorption performance than that without \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene (Figure 5e). As a result, the former gains more stable PCE performance exceeding 1200 hours. Obviously, the optical absorption of the both PSCs almost keeps the same at \(\mathrm{t}=0\ \mathrm{h}\), demonstrating that the introduction of \(\mathrm{Au/Nb}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\mathrm{x}}\) does not affect the absorption. However, plasmonic local heating induced by Au NPs can significantly suppress the segregation of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and enhance the stability of perovskite film by releasing the residual strain, which was ignored by the initial reference but was previously clarified by Li et al.[129] Moreover, large exciton binding energy within the PSCs will also make obstacles for effective electron–hole separation, limiting the PCE of PSCs. Zhang and his coauthors incorporated plasmonic \(\mathrm{AuNP@SiO}_{2}\) into mesoporous
\(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) ETL of the PSCs (Figure 5f) to regulate the exciton binding energy. Figure 5g shows the absorption spectra of Au and \(\mathrm{Au@SiO}_{2}\), demonstrating that the introduction of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) had little influence on the absorption performance of Au. However, the incorporation of \(\mathrm{Au@SiO}_{2}\) in PSCs had an obvious PCE improvement compared with the control one (Figure 5h). Time-resolved PL measurements (Figure 5i) indicated that PSC with the \(\mathrm{Au@SiO}_{2}\) NPs showed an improved PL quenching rate compared with that without the \(\mathrm{Au@SiO}_{2}\) NPs, indicating that the reduction of exciton binding energy will increase the photocurrent and thus enhance the PCE. Yu and colleagues also verified this.[174] They introduced \(\mathrm{Ag}\) NPs in \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) coated 3D orchid-like \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (\(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ag@OC-TiO}_{2}\)) nanostructure into the ETL of PSCs (Figure 5j) to enhance their performance. The presentation of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) NPs significantly improved the light absorption (Figure 5k), while also slightly enlarged the interfacial transport resistance (Figure 5l). However, the overall performance of PSCs was obviously improved, probably because the enhanced light absorption improved the photocurrent, as well as local heating reduced the exciton binding energy and promoted the carrier-transport dynamics.
Local heating also has many significant applications in other fields.[107] One of the most rapidly emerging fields is pyrocalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation. Pyrocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production refers to the process that the temperature change rate causes electron-hole separation for producing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) by pyroelectric materials. You et al. fabricated an \(\mathrm{Au/BaTiO}_{3}\) composite materials (Figure 5o), where Au was applied as local heating source under the illumination and \(\mathrm{BaTiO}_{3}\) was used for the pyroelectric material to produce efficient charges for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production.[126] Through different control experiments (Figure 5p), \(\mathrm{Au/BaTiO}_{3}\) exhibited the best \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production performance, owing to the synergistic effect between the in situ Au plasmonic local heating and pyrocatalysis. According to the simulation results (Figure 5q), the highest temperature on the \(\mathrm{BaTiO}_{3}\) heated by the local heating can increase about \(90\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in 25 ns and decay in 45 ns at one local heating cycle, enabling multiple cold-hot cycles in one second and thus achieving a good \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production performance.
## 2.3. Light–Dielectric Nanostructure Interaction
Periodic dielectric photonic structures are able to redirect the incident light into or out of the PSC devices, offering an excellent platform for manipulating the photon behaviors.[68,73,179–181] Due to the different working principles of various devices, specific optical properties of the dielectric photonic structures can be modulated by reasonable light management.
For perovskite, Si and other SCs, it is generally expected that solar light is absorbed as much as possible by the active layer. However, due to the impedance mismatch between air and planar multilayer structure of SCs, the reflection will cause a large amount of light energy loss on a smooth surface (Figure 6a). The reflected intensity can be estimated by the formula: \(R=[(n_{1}-n_{2})/(n_{1}+n_{2})]^{2}\),[73] where \(R\) is the reflectivity, \(n_{1}\) and \(n_{2}\) stand for the refractive indexes of air and medium, respectively. According to this formula, when the \(n_{1}\) is close to \(n_{2}\), light reflection loss would reach a minimum. Therefore, reasonable designs of dielectric photonic nanostructures are efficient for reducing
the loss of light. General light management strategies include moth-eye antireflective nanostructure for increasing light trapping (Figure 6b), low-aspect-ratio nanostructure for enhancing light scattering (Figure 6c), as well as Fabry–Pérot (F–P) resonance (Figure 6d), grating resonance (Figure 6e), and whispering gallery resonance (Figure 6f) to extend the light path length.
Various light management strategies are capable of being applied in PSCs to improve their performance. As a typical light-trapping resonance nanostructure, the preparation of periodic gratings tends to use expensive facilities (such as lithography machine) and require a complicated process. To overcome this issue, Wang et al.[177] developed a facile and cost-effective method that applied a commercial DVD disc to design and fabricate moiré-PSCs (Figure 6g). The moiré-PSC structure consisted of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) grating as the ETL and perovskite grating as the light absorbing material. By changing the angle between the two gratings (Figure 6h), the light-trapping properties of moiré-PSCs were highly modulated. The moiré structure of perovskite film is presented in Figure 6i. Through the unique design, the moiré structure not only promoted charge extraction and decreased surface electron–hole recombination due to the improved specific area, but also exhibited an increased \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and a big \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) (Figure 6j), resulting in an efficiency approaching to \(20\%\). It is not limited to regulate light-trapping property within the PSCs, but also can be tuned on the surface transparency of substrate. Kim et al.[178] simply placed moth-eye structured polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) film on the ITO glass of PSCs (Figure 6k) to improve the light capture capability. The light-harvesting structure was presented in Figure 6l, where moth-eye PDMS film has a periodicity of \(1000\ \mathrm{nm}\). The transmission measurement experiments showed that moth-eye structure with a periodicity of \(300\ \mathrm{nm}\) exhibited the highest transmission among the different samples due to the appropriate gradient refractive index (Figure 6m). Moreover, the parabolic shape of the surface gradually altered the refractive index at the interface of the PDMS surface and air, which effectively minimized the reflection caused by the Fresnel effect at the interface. As a result, the PSC covered by the optimal moth-eye structure achieved the highest efficiency of \(21.93\%\) (Figure 6n). In short, the periodic dielectric structure can be applied for improving the efficiency of PSCs not only on the surface, but also within the bulk.
## 3. New Nanophotonics Approaches for Enhancing the Efficiency and Stability of PSCs
## 3.1. Plasmonic Local Heating
The local heating effect can effectively heal the defect and release the strain in the perovskite. It is acknowledged that the as-cast perovskite is initially maintained in a metastable state, containing complex intermediate-phases.[183,184] The transformation from wet film to stable perovskite phase requires different thermal treatments (such as annealing, microlaser heating and lo
cal heating, Figure 7a) to remove residual solvent as well as release the residual strain. However, pinholes and defects can also be generated within the bulk and surface of perovskite film during such a process, limiting the performance of devices. To avoid these issues during the annealing process of perovskite, Kim et al. developed a fast laser crystallization strategy by performing the perovskite phase-transition based on the laser photothermal effect,[182] as shown in Figure 7b. Under the illumination of a nondamage near-infrared (NIR) laser \(\lambda=1064\ \mathrm{nm}\), high-quality perovskite film with large grain size can be obtained, as shown in Figure 7c. A higher laser intensity can further enhance the grain size and achieve comparable performance with the traditional annealing method (Figure 7d,e), which shows large potential in the fabrication of large area and flexible PSC devices. However, these thermal annealing strategies are unable to diminish the nanoscale defects and pinholes inside perovskite film. Recently, it is demonstrated that the plasmonic NPs can not only strengthen light absorption, but also produce local heating effect to effectively heal the defect and release the strain in the perovskite.[129] Jen et al. introduced the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-coated Au nanorods \((\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2})\) into perovskite film and the plasmonic local heating effect was able to in situ release the residual strain of the perovskite film. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image shows that the average size of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) cores is \(8.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) in diameter and \(40\ \mathrm{nm}\) in length, and the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) shells have a thickness of \(15\ \mathrm{nm}\), as shown in Figure 7f. Coupling optical and thermal transfer simulations demonstrate a temperature increase of \(\approx3.8\ \mathrm{K}\) under one-sun illumination (Figure 7g). From the residual strain analysis, it is found that perovskite film with \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanorod shows significantly decreased strain, and the distribution of strain becomes more homogenous with perovskite film since the heat generated from \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanorod compensate the thermal expansion differences and slow down the shrinkage from surface to bottom (Figure 7h,i). As a result, the intrinsic stability of the resulting nonencapsulated PSCs is greatly improved, retaining \(84\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1000\ \mathrm{h}\) aging under continuous illumination at \(65\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glovebox (Figure 7j).
## 3.2. Surface Plasmon Modulation
Plasmonic metal nanostructures have been considered as one of the most popular approaches of light trapping in the PV community due to the following effects:[74] 1) localized near-field effect; 2) light scattering for broadband optical absorption enhancement; and 3) energy transfer between metal nanostructures and the active layer. It has been evidenced that the performance enhancement was demonstrated in various plasmonic PV devices, such as Si, organic semiconductor, and DSSCs. Plasmonic nanostructures as light-trapping and scattering centers can be placed at different positions in the device to enhance light absorption (Figure 8a). First, metallic NPs with an average diameter larger than \(50\ \mathrm{nm}\), used as subwavelength scattering elements,
can be placed at the surface of SC (Figure 8a1), so that they can freely couple incident light into the active layer. Second, metallic NPs with diameters of \(5{-}20\;\mathrm{nm}\) can be used as the antennas in the perovskite layer, in which the plasmonic near-field is coupled with the semiconductor to increase the effective absorption cross-section of active layer (Figure 8a2). Third, the periodically grating arrays can be integrated at the back surface of the active layer (Figure 8a3). It can couple incident light into SPP modes at the metal/semiconductor interface to largely boost the light absorption within the active layer. Generally, these metal nanostruc
tures enhance PV performance mainly through light-absorption enhancement in a broadband wavelength range.
Plasmonic metal NPs can be synthesized in different compositions with a variety of shapes and coatings, such as spheres, rods, stars, cubes, and other irregular polyhedrons, as well as obtaining numerous dielectric coatings (e.g., \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\)) on the surface for better chemical stability.[98,99,107] Figure 8b displays the high-magnification SEM images of three types of metal nanostructures, such as the b1) silica-coated Au spheres, b2) silica-coated Au rods, and b3) silver cubes. In practice, both shape and
coating materials can influence the optical absorption effect of PVs. Míguez et al.[186] simulated the plasmonic metal material-dependent electric field distribution under the excitation wavelength at \(750\ \mathrm{nm}\), as shown in Figure 8c. It was found that the electric field enhancement for silver was significantly higher than Au or Al particles. They also estimated the influence of silica shell coating on Au spheres \((\mathsf{A u@S i O}_{2})\) on optical absorption effects of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite by varying the thickness of coating, as shown in Figure 8c. The simulation results demonstrated that a \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) silica layer allowed an enhanced reinforcement effect for optical absorption. Although the light absorption of \(\mathsf{A u@S i O}_{2}\)-coated perovskite film outperforms the pristine perovskite, a thicker dielectric protective layer (e.g., \(25\ \mathrm{nm}\)) will lead to a strong localization of the optical fields in the shell, diminishing the absorption of active layer. Wu et al.[187] introduced an Au nanorod–NP dimers into the perovskite film to investigate the LSPR effect of the Au dimer on the optical absorption of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{x}}\) perovskite. The TEM image (Figure 8e) of the Au dimer shows that each configuration is composed of a nanorod (length: \(75\pm7\ \mathrm{nm}\), diameter: \(18\pm2\ \mathrm{nm}\)) and a nanosphere (diameter: \(30\pm3\ \mathrm{nm}\)). The authors simulated the near-field intensity distribution of Au dimer embedded in perovskite film at a wavelength of \(650\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 8f). An obvious enhancement in near-field intensity has been achieved in the vicinity of the Au dimer. Figure 8g plots the extinction spectrum of Au dimer, where a plasmon resonance band at \(560\ \mathrm{nm}\) can significantly boost the absorption of perovskite film for increasing photocurrent.
Extending the plasmonic resonance band is an effective method for further improving the light harvesting efficiency and maximizing the performance of SCs. Lei et al.[188] synthesized a series of \(\mathrm{Au@Ag}\) core–shell nanocuboids with tailoring the Ag coating thickness from 10 to \(25\ \mathrm{nm}\) by a wet chemistry method. The optical images show the color changes of the \(\mathrm{Au@Ag}\) core–shell nanocuboid colloids solution from red to blue and finally to purple, as shown in Figure 8h. The extinction spectra (Figure 8i) of the Au nanorods show two absorption peaks located at 531 and \(852\ \mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the transverse and longitudinal LSPR modes, respectively. Upon applying Ag coating (Figure 8i), four LSPR peaks (numbered as peaks #1–4 from low to high energies sequentially) are generated simultaneously. Taking \(\mathrm{Au@Ag}\) \((15\ \mathrm{nm})\) nanocuboid for an example, the peak #1 at \(\approx631\ \mathrm{nm}\) can be ascribed to the longitudinal dipolar plasmon mode and the peak #2 \((\approx468\ \mathrm{nm})\) to the transverse dipolar plasmon mode of the \(\mathrm{Au@Ag}\) nanocuboids and two higher-energy peaks at 410 and \(341\ \mathrm{nm}\) can be ascribed to the octupolar plasmon modes of the Ag shell. As a result, the extinction intensities at 480 and \(616\ \mathrm{nm}\) are 5 and 12 times higher than the Au nanorods, respectively. It is convenient to employ the plasmonic nanocuboids in different layers of the PV device, such as at the bottom, on the top, and embedding in the bulk, as shown in Figure 8j for the configurations #1, #2, #3, and #4, respectively. It was demonstrated that the PV performance with configuration #4 (Figure 8k) showed the best performance due to the LSPR enhanced absorption and the scattering effect. Furthermore, a more significant enhancement
in performance and IPCE were observed by combining the LSPR and scattering effects, as shown in Figure 8l.
## 3.3. Periodic Dielectric Nanostructure for Efficient Light Management
Generally speaking, most PV materials have high-refractive indexes (e.g., \(>2.2\)), implying a large portion of reflection according to the Section 2.3. Optical resonance can significantly enhance the light absorption, thus benefiting for the performance of SCs. Figure 9a displays a schematic illustration of sphere-type nanostructure. When the dielectric nanostructure is applied to the PV film, the photons will generate four kinds of coupled optical modes, including Mie resonance, F–P resonance, waveguide resonance, and diffracted grating resonance modes, as labeled as 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively, in the Figure 9a. The angle-dependent spatial absorption profile within the PV film is provided in Figure 9b, where the labels 1–4 specify illumination conditions (angle and wavelength) and how the four resonance modes are excited under these conditions. Figure 9c shows the spatial profiles of electromagnetic energy density corresponding to the Mie resonance, F-P resonance, waveguide resonance, and diffracted resonance inside a SC, under the incident wavelength at 880, 1031, 946, and \(1011\;\mathsf{nm}\), respectively. The different field distributions correspond to different resonance modes. Hence, spatial electromagnetic intensity distribution within the SCs can be used to analyze optical resonance modes and light-trapping capacity, which are highly dependent on the incident angle and wavelength of light. Generally, the symmetrical hexagonal distribution of electromagnetic energy density represents Mie resonance inside the nanostructure (Figure 9c1). A F–P resonance is formed in the active layer of SCs because a Fabry-Perot cavity is constructed between the high-index nanostructure surface and metal back-reflection layer (Figure 9c2). The field distribution of waveguide resonance mode (Figure 9c3) shows the periodic intensity variations in the plane of the active layer, and this resonance mode is generated by the periodic
conditions (angle and wavelength) and how the four resonance modes are excited under these conditions. Figure 9c shows the spatial profiles of electromagnetic energy density corresponding to the Mie resonance, F-P resonance, waveguide resonance, and diffracted resonance inside a SC, under the incident wavelength at 880, 1031, 946, and \(1011\;\mathsf{nm}\) , respectively. The different field distributions correspond to different resonance modes. Hence, spatial electromagnetic intensity distribution within the SCs can be used to analyze optical resonance modes and lighttrapping capacity, which are highly dependent on the incident angle and wavelength of light. Generally, the symmetrical hexagonal distribution of electromagnetic energy density represents Mie resonance inside the nanostructure (Figure 9c1). A F–P resonance is formed in the active layer of SCs because a FabryPerot cavity is constructed between the high-index nanostructure surface and metal back-reflection layer (Figure 9c2). The field distribution of waveguide resonance mode (Figure 9c3) shows the periodic intensity variations in the plane of the active layer, and this resonance mode is generated by the periodic
patterned arrays that act as a diffracted grating structure. The diffracted grating resonance (Figure 9c4) mode can redirect the incident light into the active layer, and its resonance effect can be extended to the underlying active layer, which confines more photons within the active layer and facilitates efficient absorption.
The diffraction-based light-trapping structure can improve photon-recycling and thus suppress voltage loss. The performance of a PV device is determined by three key parameters, including \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\), \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\), and fill factor \((FF)\). Current research mainly focuses on the nanostructure-induced \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) improvement of SCs. In practice, nanostructure design is also beneficial for boosting the electrical performance of PV devices (e.g., \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and \(FF\)). For a direct-bandgap PV material, the absorbed photons within an active layer may suffer from huge radiative decay process to re-emit
photons. However, these emitted photons have a high probability to be re-absorbed, which is the so-called the photon-recycling effect. The photon-recycling effect can boost the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) by increasing the splitting of quasi-Fermi level. For a grating nanostructure, these re-emitted photons can couple with the available propagating diffraction modes inside the active layer to maximize the photon recycling process, as shown in Figure 9d,e. Peters et al.[193] derived the maximum optical path enhancement for a diffraction-based light-trapping structure following \(\alpha_{ope} = 893\,n\), where \(\alpha_{ope}\) stands for optical path enhancement, and \(n\) is the refractive index of active layer. The concentration of diffraction-based optical length increasement on the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of PV can be described: \(\Delta V_{oc}^{PR} = \left(\frac{k_{B}T}{q}\right)\ln(893n)\), where \(\Delta V_{OC}^{PR}\), \(k_{B}\), \(T\), \(q\) and \(n\) represent for \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) enhanced by photon cycling, Boltzmann constant, absolute
temperature in K, elementary charge and the refractive index of the absorber, respectively. For a perfect process of the absorption of re-emitted photons, grating-based nanostructure can enlarge the \( V_{OC} \) for over \( 200~\mathrm{mV} \) in a PSC \( (n=2.5 \) for perovskite). Furthermore, dielectric double-array cone-like broadband nanostructure can be designed to obtain strong light scattering and photon-recycling effect simultaneously. This kind of design has already been successfully implemented in GaAs SCs to boost the PV performance, showing the potential for utilization in PSCs.
With the development of micro/nano technology, these nanostructures can not only be integrated on the top layer of SCs, but also embedded in each functional layer of the device, including the electrodes, ETL, HTL, and active layer. Song et al.[191] used commercial optical discs (CD-R and DVD-R) to imprint a micro-nano photonic structure into the perovskite active layer of SC. The masters of CD and DVD with respective \( \approx 1.5 \) and \( \approx 0.75~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) in periodicity, and 0.5 and \( 0.25~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) in line width, are shown in Figure 9f. As a result, the grating within the perovskite film with a periodicity of \( 0.7{-}0.8~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) enabled first-order diffraction in the absorption range of perovskite, which significantly increased the light absorption and current density of PSCs. From the electrical perspective, constructing periodically ordered micro-nanostructures on the perovskite active layer can effectively increase the heterojunction contact area, which is beneficial for carrier separation and transportation to diminish the bulk and interface nonradiative recombination losses. Compared to the pristine PSCs, the diffraction-grating perovskite devices obtained higher \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \), \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \), and \( FF \), and finally the PCE was improved from \( 16.71\% \) to \( 19.71\% \).
Nanostructures have also been extensively applied to various tandem PV cells for better performance.[192] Figure \( 9\mathrm{g} \) shows the cross-sectional SEM images of three kinds of perovskite/Si tandem nanostructure design for planar (g1), sinusoidal nanostructures at the top (g2), and nanotextured + dielectric buffer reflection layer device configuration (g3). The sinusoidal nanostructure at the front surface of the silicon subcell has a hexagonal lattice with a period of \( 750~\mathrm{nm} \) and a peak-to-valley height of \( \approx 300~\mathrm{nm} \) (Figure 9g4). The RDBL comprises a \( \mathrm{SiO}_{2} \) buffer layer between the transparent conducting oxide and the silver back-reflector, reducing parasitic absorption losses to further improve the current density in the silicon bottom cell by around \( 0.3~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2} \). By combining nanotextures with RDBL in one perovskite/Si tandem device, the developed nanostructured tandem PV obtained a certified PCE of \( 29.80\% \).
## 3.4. Radiative Cooling Design
Except for improving the optoelectronic performance of PV devices, photonic nanostructures can also be used to manage their thermal dissipation by integrating SCs with radiative coolers. It is acknowledged that the theoretical limiting efficiency of single-junction PV device is around \( 33\% \) under AM 1.5G irradiation, meaning that a large portion of the solar energy absorbed by the SCs will be converted into unwanted heat inside the device. And the thermal effect is detrimental for the performance and stability of PVs. Recently, passive radiative cooling technology based on nanophotonics has attracted extensive attention by reflecting sunlight and radiating heat to the ultracold outer space
(Figure 10a), and therefore can effectively cool the PV devices without any electrical input.[195,197,198] As depicted in Figure 10b, the spectral response of nanostructured radiative cooler need to be accurately designed from solar to infrared bands (e.g., 0.3– \( 25~\upmu\mathrm{m} \)). For example, for the radiative coolers of crystalline-Si SCs, the essential considerations include: 1) a maximum transmittance at SC absorption band to ensure the performance of SCs (e.g., \( 0.3{-}1.1~\upmu\mathrm{m} \)); 2) high reflectivity in \( 1.1{-}4~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) of solar irradiation band to avoid parasitic heat absorption inside the SC; 3) high thermal emissivity in the rest infrared range (e.g., \( 4{-}25~\upmu\mathrm{m} \)) to perform radiative cooling. Fan et al.[195] experimentally designed a silica photonic crystal structure with a periodicity of \( 6~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) and \( 10~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) air holes in depth, as shown in Figure 10c. By mounting the designed photonic cooling structures on the bare Si SCs, it not only significantly altered the solar absorption properties (Figure 10d), but also achieved very high emissivity in the wavelength range of \( 6{-}25~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) for radiative cooling, as shown in Figure 10e, where the black, blue, and red curves show the measured emissivity/absorptivity for the bare silicon, silicon integrated with the planar silica layer, and silicon integrated with the silica photonic crystal, respectively. Considering the actual environmental situation during daytime, the bare structure with the silica photonic crystal showed a lower surface temperature of \( 13\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) than the bare SCs (Figure 10f), which promises to improve SC efficiency.[199] Fan et al. further proposed a PDMS photonic structure applied as radiative cooling layer to lower the operation temperature and improve the efficiency of PV devices.[196] By introducing the radiative cooling layer, the emissivity in the range from 8 to \( 25~\upmu\mathrm{m} \) of SC was significantly improved (Figure 10g). Meanwhile, the surface temperature of SC also remarkably decreased about \( 11.5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) compared with that without a cooling layer (Figure 10h), resulting apparent efficiency improvements in different types of SCs (Figure 10i). However, more in-depth research into employing radiative cooling to improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs are still missing, thus more efforts should be paid to improve the current situation.[200] In short, radiative cooling technology for decreasing the surface temperature of PSCs as well as other PVs presents huge potentials for improving their efficiency and long-term operational stability.
## 3.5. Semitransparent and Smart Window Designs
Considering aesthetic factors and practical applications, nanophotonic technology has been widely used to design multifunctional PV devices for multiscenario applications, such as colorful and semitransparent SC for building integrated PV devices. Inspired by the cuticle of a beetle (Figure 11a), Choi et al.[201] developed the colorful PSCs with tuneable R, G, and B colors by integrating the liquid crystalline ChRFs, as shown in Figure 11b. Generally, reflective wavelengths determine the hue, bandwidth affects the PCE, and reflectance magnitude limits the chromaticity and purity of color. However, these visual properties can be simultaneously modulated by tuning the contents of a chiral dopant, birefringence of the host liquid crystalline molecules and ChRF structure. Compared with the opaque PSCs, the colorful perovskite with R-, G-, and B-ChRF2 showed PCE values of \( 16.3\% \), \( 17.8\% \), and \( 19.5\% \), respectively, corresponding to PCE losses of \( 17\% \), \( 13\% \), and \( 9\% \). Based on the
nonperiodic \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) multilayers, Do et al.[202] developed three-color (R, G, and B) PSCs with a minimized PCE loss \((<\!10\%)\). It is well known that 1D multinanolayers show an optical property variation with respect to viewing angle. Therefore, it is expected that the RGB-NBRF films will display different reflective colors depending on where the viewer stands. Figure 11c shows the reflective color changes with angular variation of the three-color multinanolayers. As the viewing angle increases, all spectra are blue-shifted and end with a pearl-like color. The PCEs of PSCs with nonperiodic RGB-NBRFs were \(18.0\%\) , \(18.6\%\) , and \(18.9\%\) , corresponding to reductions of only \(10\%\) , \(7\%\) , and \(6\%\) of PCE values, respectively, compared to a black control PSC \((20.1\%)\). These colorful PSCs not only present the desired color, but maximally retain the optoelectronic conversion efficiency.
In particular, semi-transparent SCs have attracted more attention for future applications, such as windows integrated into the exterior of buildings and automobiles. However, the trade-off between PV efficiency and optical transparency should be consid
ered and evaluated carefully. The conventional approach is to reduce the thickness of perovskite layer to attain the desirable high transparency. To achieve colorful semi-transparent SCs, the nanostructure should be designed accurately. Park et al.[203] proposed the semi-transparent multiple color PSCs based on the microcavities effect. Figure 11d shows the configuration of designed colorful SC devices. As silver presents the highest reflectivity and zinc sulfide (ZnS) exhibits a high index of refraction with high transparency in the visible wavelength range, they were selected as the metallic mirror and cavity material, respectively, where the optical microcavities in a dielectric–metal–dielectric–metal configuration with a phase-compensating dielectric overlay were integrated at the cathode. Figure 11e shows the measured transmission spectra of the colored semi-transparent PSCs at normal incidence along with photographs of the fabricated samples. Moon et al.[204] designed a 1D nanostructured PSCs employing AAO as the scaffold layer. By accurately tailoring the pore size in the AAO, the volume occupied by the perovskite layer can
be effectively tuned to achieve the desired color. The pictures of the nanopillar-structured perovskite devices with different AAO template/perovskite layer \( t_{\mathrm{perov}} = 90 \) / \( t_{\mathrm{AAO}} = 150 \), \( t_{\mathrm{perov}} = 150 \) / \( t_{\mathrm{AAO}} = 220 \), \( t_{\mathrm{perov}} = 220 \) / \( t_{\mathrm{AAO}} = 300 \) is depicted in Figure 11f. The average visible transmissions were \( 45.4\% \), \( 41.5\% \), \( 37.4\% \) for perovskite layers with thicknesses of 150, 220, and \( 280 \, \mathrm{nm} \), respectively, with the color variation of the devices from yellow to brown, as depicted in Figure 5b. Despite a high transparency, the PCEs of these devices could be retained as high as \( 8.49\% \), \( 10.26\% \), \( 10.81\% \).
Reasonable nanophotonics design can simultaneously ensure high PCE of PSCs, aesthetic purposes, and smart thermal management, which indirectly gain additional value to the semitransparent perovskite window. Although colorful and semitransparent PSCs can respectively offer aesthetic value and efficient light manipulation, as well as being capable of converting light into electricity at the same time, both of them are unable to achieve smart thermal management for minimizing building energy consumption. In order to overcome this challenge, Li et al. group developed one kind of self-powered smart perovskite window,[205] whose optical transmission can be shifted between dark and bright modes via a switch circuit. Figure 11g shows the working principle of self-powered smart perovskite window in the bright state, in which the authors took advantage of the reversible deposition-dissolving of Cu-Bi bimetal to tune the optical transmittance by manually shifting the anode and cathode of the PSC. The visible transmittance of the smart window in both modes are presented in Figure 11h. The visible transmittance at \( 550 \, \mathrm{nm} \) showed about \( 50\% \) adjustability between the bright and dark states. The inset photographs of Figure 11h also demonstrate the excellent optical transmission tunability of the integrated self-powered smart window. When the device was evaluated under repeatedly brightening and darkening processes, the smart window that was only powered by the PSC could be reversibly switched into bright and dark modes without an obvious decline in transmission (Figure 11i), demonstrating its excellent stability. However, switching modes manually makes the self-powered smart perovskite window rather inconvenient and not very "smart". In order to overcome it, Xia and co-authors fabricated a high-performance semi-transparent PSC (Figure 11j) integrated with multiresponsive liquid crystal/polymer composite (LCPC) films for smarter and more energy-saving window.[94] In the daytime (Figure 11k), the smart perovskite window exhibited a light scattering state with low transmission to block most of the light entering the building, achieving the thermal regulation and thus energy-saving purposes. Moreover, it could show a PCE of \( \sim 18\% \) in the daytime mode. While the PSC could hardly work in the night mode, it could still automatically change into a highly transparent state for providing a nice view of beautiful evening scenery.
## 4. Conclusions and Outlook
Nanophotonics approaches, as a class of the most effective means of light management, are of crucial importance for improving both the efficiency and stability of PSCs. In this review, we have systematically analyzed and summarized the fundamentals and applications of two types of nanophotonic systems, namely plasmonic metal nanostructures and periodic dielectric nanostruc
tures. We discussed the excitation of plasmons from various metal nanostructures and explained the relaxation processes involved. Next, the latest applications based on radiative decay and nonradiative decay (such as hot carrier generation and relaxation, and local heating) are summarized. Finally, we reviewed the state-of-the-art and potential nanophotonics methods that are applied to improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs. Especially, radiative cooling technology that can reduce the operating temperature of PSCs, and therefore inhibit the halide segregation and release of residual strain, is a promising way to enhance the long-term operational stability for speeding up the commercialization of PSCs. Unfortunately, radiative cooling PSCs are rarely reported. The reasons may be because radiative cooling layer on PSCs usually requires high dielectric nanostructured polymer, which may decrease the PCE, increase the cost of SC, and induce polymer degradation during the long-term operation. Therefore, high transmission, low cost and high stability inorganic radiative cooling layers, such as \( \mathrm{SiO}_{2} \) and hexagonal boron nitride, can be excellent candidates.
The new nanophotonics methods provide a lot of new opportunities for high-efficiency, durable, low-risk and sustainable PSCs. However, more efforts should be invested toward the large-scale processes to solve the commercialization challenges of PSCs, e.g., 1) higher efficiency and stability against moisture, air, heat and light with the assistance of novel nanophotonics designs; 2) optimization of plasmonic nanostructures as the key roles in ETL, HTL, perovskite layer as well as grating electrode for hot-carrier generation, local heating and photon cycling to further enhance the efficiency and stability;[206] reasonable nanophotonics design, such as Si/perovskite tandem SCs,[207] to harvest more photons and reduce thermalization loss to boost the efficiency; 4) developing high-performance radiative cooler with high transmission in solar spectrum range and high emission in atmospheric window to cool the temperature, and thus to extend the long-term operational lifetime; and 5) integrating the both advantages of semi-transparent PSCs and the thermochromic glass to realize smart windows for energy-saving buildings.
So far, the maximum efficiency of single-junction PSCs has reached to \( 26.1\% \),[208–210] one step closer to the Shockley–Queisser (S–Q) limit of \( 32.7\% \).[211,212] A gap of \( 6.6\% \) between the experimental value and theoretical value demonstrates that there still exists space to improve the efficiency. Nanophotonics methods, such as embedding an ultrathin dielectric interlayer between the absorber and charge-transporting layers to enhance light trapping,[213] may offer new inspiration to significantly improve the efficiency. Besides, plasmonic dynamic characteristics, including plasmon-induced radiative decay and nonradiative decay, have shown capability to improve PSC efficiency and stability through facilitating photon recycling, hot carrier relaxation and local heating processes. On the other hand, the largest challenge for commercializing PSCs is to keep a high efficiency and simultaneously long-term operational stability on a large-scale module level.[53] It is notable that that the temperature effect deteriorate the long-term stability of PSCs, which is an unavoidable problem during SC operation and can induce the formation of defects, and strain accumulation. Cooling the PSC during the operation is an effective way to extend its lifetime. Therefore, radiative cooling enabled by the periodic dielectric nanostructures can effectively decrease the operating temperature of SCs.[196,214] However, the
trade-off between the efficiency and cooling effect have a long way to go. In addition, other nanophotonics approaches, including reducing radiative recombination, improving the transportation dynamics of hot-carrier, restoring the intrinsic defects by local heating, and reasonable light and thermal managements by the plasmonic and dielectric nanostructures, may also offer new opportunities for higher efficiency and more stable PSCs.
Finally, we highlight that nanophotonics methods are not only capable of improving the efficiency and stability of PSCs, but also can provide additional aesthetic value for the end users,[215,216] and simultaneously can be utilized in semi-transparent PSCs for energy-saving smart windows.[94,217] Moreover, the applications of nanophotonics are not confined to the field of PSCs alone, as they can be extended to various fields where nanostructures have the capability to modulate desired optical applications, such as imaging,[120,218–220] grating,[221] (non)linear optics,[135] light-emitting diode (LED),[222] super lens,[223] radiative cooling,[198] smart windows,[217] antireflection,[224] photothermal conversion,[225] photochemical transformation,[226–228] and biosensing.[229]
